[b]A replacement for Perspective: Paths.[/b] Remove it, load your save, and the areas you painted with it come back as Neat Edges' hard edge area, tile for tile, with the painting tools on the Zone tab where its were. The original and the continued version save their areas the same way, so either one carries over.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/beverage/neat-edges/main/media/cards/banner-what-it-does.png[/img]

RimWorld fades every terrain one tile into whatever it touches, and the receiving tile has no say in it. That is why a stone terrace meeting open ground looks like it is dissolving at the rim. Neat Edges gives a tile that say, from the Floors and Zone tabs of the Architect menu.

[list]
[*][b]Hard edge area[/b]: whole tiles, painted by dragging the way you paint a home area. Free and instant, and nothing is drawn on the map. Where Perspective: Paths stopped terrain fading into a painted tile but let the tile's own terrain fade out onto its neighbours, Neat Edges stops both, so a painted area's border comes out crisper.
[*][b]Hard edge[/b]: one edge of one tile, which a painted area cannot do. Rotate it to pick the side, and stack several on a tile for any combination. Invisible, and free of materials.
[*][b]Floor borders[/b]: visible trim in wood, stone or metal that takes paint, as a straight strip, a corner, a runner down both sides of a corridor, an end cap, a frame around a single tile, and an inside corner for where a border turns around a jutting wall. Each one hardens the edges it covers.
[/list]

Where two hardened edges meet, the corner between them is sealed too, so a run ends square instead of the fringe curling around its last tile. The overlay toggle on the bottom-right row shows every marker and the painted area, since the marker draws nothing and the toggle is the only way to find one again.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/beverage/neat-edges/main/media/cards/banner-compatibility.png[/img]

Keeping Perspective: Paths? While it is installed, Neat Edges hides its own area tools and leaves whole-tile painting to it, and the marker and the floor borders work alongside it.

Works with any floor from any mod, keeps Dub's Paint Shop colours, and works alongside vanilla bridges. Requires [url=https://steamcommunity.com/workshop/filedetails/?id=2009463077]Harmony[/url]. You can add it to a running save. Before removing it, use Clear hard edge area on every map you painted: a save that still holds the area loses the map's whole area list, home area included, and clearing the last painted tile removes the area itself. Markers and borders can stay; RimWorld drops each one with a load error, and floors and terrain are untouched. Other mods can make their own decorations harden edges through the extension the floor borders carry, with no dependency beyond a [i]MayRequire[/i].

Removing Perspective: Paths on its own is not safe: it adds its zone to every map, painted or not, the game gives no way to delete it, and a save that still holds one loses the map's whole area list, home area included. With Neat Edges installed, those zones load as its own instead.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/beverage/neat-edges/main/media/cards/banner-recommended-with.png[/img]

[b][url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3531114826]Flooring Floors[/url][/b]. Neat Edges ships no floors of its own, and Flooring Floors adds hundreds, in stone, wood, metal, carpet and precious materials: the floors this mod exists to edge. It has a floor border of its own, in stone only and as a single straight piece. A corner there is two strips laid over each other, and terrain still fades in past the band. Neat Edges' borders come in wood, stone or metal, with corner, inside corner, runner, end cap and frame pieces that meet as one unbroken line, and each one stops the fade on the edges it covers.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/beverage/neat-edges/main/media/cards/banner-source.png[/img]

The painted area follows Owlchemist's [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=2934666296]Perspective: Paths[/url], which solved the whole-tile case first.

MIT licensed, with the design notes and a regression harness in the repository. Written with AI assistance.

